        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A water heater is a sealed steel tank full of water being heated by a gas burner or electric elements. If the controls fail, the temperature and pressure inside keep climbing, and the T&P valve is what stops that from ending with a ruptured tank. When it drips, it is almost always telling you something true about your pressure or your plumbing. The fix is to listen to it, not to silence it.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="what-it-does" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What the T&P Valve Does
          </h2>

          <p>
            The temperature and pressure relief valve, usually called the T&P valve, threads into an opening on the top or upper side of a tank water heater. It has a small lever on top, an outlet on the side with a pipe running down toward the floor, and on the tank side a long probe that reaches into the hottest water at the top of the tank. Residential valves are typically set to open at 150 psi or 210°F, whichever comes first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those two numbers are chosen for a reason. Most residential tanks are rated for a working pressure of 150 psi, so the valve opens before the tank's limit. And 210°F sits just below the boiling point of water at sea level. Water in a closed tank under pressure can be heated well above 212°F without boiling, and if the tank ruptures, that superheated water flashes to steam instantly and expands violently. The temperature side of the valve prevents that condition from ever building.
          </p>

          <p>
            In normal operation, the valve does nothing at all. Your thermostat holds the water at 120 to 140°F, and household pressure sits well below 150 psi. The valve only opens if something goes wrong: a thermostat or gas valve sticks on, a pressure-reducing valve fails, or thermal expansion has nowhere to go. When it opens, it dumps hot water through the discharge pipe until the condition drops back below the set point, then closes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plumbing codes require one on every storage water heater, and the valve must be listed for the heater's input rating, with its probe in the top portion of the tank. A replacement valve has to match the original's pressure and temperature setting and be rated for at least the heater's BTU or wattage input. The rating is stamped on the valve body or a metal tag attached to it, so read it before buying a replacement.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers That Matter</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">150 psi</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical opening pressure</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">210°F</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical opening temperature</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">80 psi</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical code max house pressure</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1 year</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Watts' manual test interval</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How to Test a T&P Valve Safely
          </h2>

          <p>
            Valve makers such as Watts tell owners to operate the lever manually at least once a year to make sure the waterways are clear, and to have the valve reinspected by a licensed plumbing contractor at least once every three years, because corrosion inside the valve cannot be seen without removing it. The manual test takes two minutes, but it involves releasing scalding water, so set it up properly.
          </p>

          <p>
            First, confirm that a discharge pipe is attached to the valve outlet and runs down to within a few inches of the floor or into a drain. If there is no pipe, do not test the valve; you would be spraying hot water across the room. Put a bucket under the end of the pipe, keep your hands and face away from it, and wear gloves. You do not need to turn off the heater for a quick test.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lift the lever and hold it for a few seconds. You should hear a rush of water and see a strong flow out of the end of the discharge pipe. Let the lever snap back down. The flow should stop completely within a few seconds. If no water comes out, the valve is inoperative and must be replaced. If water keeps dribbling after you release the lever, lift and release it once or twice more to flush any debris off the seat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be honest with yourself about an older valve. On a heater that has gone many years without a test, mineral deposits can glue the seat, and opening it can dislodge debris that stops it from sealing properly again. That is not a reason to skip the test, because a valve that cannot open is far worse than one that drips. It is a reason to test it when you have time to buy a replacement if needed, rather than on a Sunday night.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SCALD WARNING</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">The discharge is hot enough to burn.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Water leaving a T&P valve can be at or near tank temperature, and during a real overheat it can be close to boiling. Never test a valve that lacks a discharge pipe, never look into the outlet while operating the lever, and keep children and pets away during the test.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="why-it-drips" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Why a T&P Valve Drips or Discharges
          </h2>

          <p>
            A T&P valve that drips or occasionally releases water is responding to one of four things: thermal expansion, high incoming pressure, excessive temperature, or a worn or fouled valve. The pattern of the dripping usually tells you which. Watch when it happens, check the pressure with a gauge, and you will typically have the answer within a day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thermal expansion is the most common cause. Water expands as it heats, by roughly 1.5 to 2 percent between cold supply temperature and typical tank temperature. In an open system, that extra water pushes back into the city main. But many homes now have a check valve, backflow preventer or pressure-reducing valve at the meter, which makes the system closed. The expanded water has nowhere to go, pressure spikes during each heating cycle, and the valve weeps. The tell is a drip that appears during or just after the burner or elements run, and a pressure gauge that climbs sharply while the heater is working.
          </p>

          <p>
            High static pressure is the second cause. If the water coming into the house is already above 80 psi, a heating cycle can push it toward the valve's limit quickly. A pressure-reducing valve that has failed or drifted is a common reason for this, and it produces a drip that may be more constant or show up at night when demand is low and street pressure rises. A hose-bib gauge tells you within seconds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Excess temperature is rarer but serious. If the water at the taps is far hotter than the thermostat setting, or the valve releases steam or very hot water, the thermostat or gas control may be stuck on. Turn the heater off immediately, using the gas control or the breaker, and call a plumber. Finally, if pressure and temperature are both normal, the valve itself is probably worn or has debris on its seat, and it needs to be replaced.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Pattern</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Likely cause</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What to check</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Drips during and after heating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thermal expansion in a closed system</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gauge reading during a heating cycle</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Steady drip, worse at night</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Street pressure above 80 psi</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Static pressure at a hose bib</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sudden hot discharge or steam</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Overheating, failed control</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Shut down, call a plumber</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Drips with normal pressure and temperature</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Worn or fouled valve seat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Replace the valve</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="fixing-the-cause" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Fixing the Cause: Pressure, Expansion and Temperature
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with a pressure gauge that threads onto a hose bib or the water heater drain valve. Many have a red tell-tale needle that records the highest reading, which is exactly what you want. Read the static pressure with no water running, then leave the gauge on through a few heating cycles or overnight and check the peak. Static readings in the 40 to 80 psi range are typical of a properly regulated home.
          </p>

          <p>
            If static pressure is above 80 psi, the cure is a pressure-reducing valve on the main line, or adjusting or replacing the one you have. The International Plumbing Code calls for one when static pressure exceeds 80 psi, and lower pressure protects every fixture, hose and appliance, not just the water heater. A PRV install is a plumbing job, typically done at the point where the main enters the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            If static pressure is normal but the peak climbs well above it during heating, you have thermal expansion. The fix is a thermal expansion tank on the cold supply, sized for the water heater's capacity and your house pressure, and pre-charged to match the static pressure. Many jurisdictions now require one wherever a check valve or PRV creates a closed system. Our guide to the <a href="/water-heater-expansion-tank/">water heater expansion tank</a> covers sizing, charging and installation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Temperature matters too. The U.S. Department of Energy suggests 120°F as a setting that saves energy and reduces scald risk for most households, and hotter settings increase both expansion and wear on the valve. If yours is set well above that and the valve weeps, turning it down is a free first step, though it is not a substitute for fixing a closed-system expansion problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="replacing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Replacing a T&P Valve
          </h2>

          <p>
            Replacing a T&P valve is within reach of a handy homeowner, but it involves a tank of hot water, so take it slowly. Buy a replacement that matches the original's pressure and temperature setting, has the same thread size, usually three-quarter inch, has a probe of suitable length for the tank, and is rated for at least the heater's input. Valves from Watts, Cash Acme and other makers list these on the packaging, and a residential valve is an inexpensive part.
          </p>

          <p>
            Turn the gas control to OFF or switch off the breaker. Close the cold water supply valve. Let the water cool for a few hours if you can, then open a hot water tap in the house to relieve pressure. Attach a hose to the drain valve and drain enough water to bring the level below the T&P opening; on a side-mounted valve that is only a few gallons, on a top-mounted valve even less. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-drain-a-water-heater/">how to drain a water heater</a> covers the hose setup.
          </p>

          <p>
            Unscrew the discharge pipe from the valve, then turn the valve counterclockwise with a pipe wrench. Older valves can be stubborn; steady force is better than jerking, and holding the tank steady keeps you from twisting the connectors. Wrap the new valve's threads with PTFE tape or apply a thread sealant rated for potable water, thread it in by hand, then tighten with a wrench so the outlet points the same way as before.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reattach the discharge pipe, close the drain valve, and open the cold supply with a hot tap still open. When water runs steadily from the tap with no air spitting, the tank is full. Only then restore gas or power, because firing an electric element in a partly empty tank burns it out instantly. Check the new valve and its threads for leaks after the first heating cycle.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the old valve was dripping because of expansion or high pressure, a new one will drip too. Replace the valve and fix the cause, or you will be back in a few weeks. And if the heater is old enough that the valve threads are badly corroded, it is worth checking how much life it has left; our guide on <a href="/how-to-tell-the-age-of-a-water-heater/">how to tell the age of a water heater</a> shows how to read the date from the serial number.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="discharge-pipe" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The Discharge Pipe: What Code Requires
          </h2>

          <p>
            The pipe attached to the T&P valve is not decoration. It routes scalding water to a safe place, and both major model codes, the International Plumbing Code and the Uniform Plumbing Code, set specific rules for it. Local codes adopt these with their own changes, so check with your building department, but the core requirements are very similar everywhere.
          </p>

          <p>
            Under the IPC, the discharge pipe must be no smaller than the valve outlet and run full size to its end. It must flow by gravity and must not be trapped, meaning no low spots where water sits and no uphill runs. It cannot have any valves or tee fittings, cannot be connected directly to a drain without an air gap, and cannot have threads at its end, which prevents anyone from capping it. It must be made of a material rated for the temperature and pressure, which rules out ordinary PVC drain pipe.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where it ends matters. The IPC allows termination to the floor, an indirect waste receptor, a drain pan or outdoors, and where it ends at the floor it must stop no more than 6 inches above the floor and at least two pipe diameters above it. The termination must be where occupants can see it and where the discharge will not injure anyone or damage the building. The UPC sets similar limits for outdoor terminations.
          </p>

          <p>
            Common violations are easy to spot. A discharge pipe that stops a foot above the floor, runs up and over a joist, reduces in size, uses a flexible plastic hose, or is threaded at the end with a cap on it all fail inspection. If you are replacing a heater, the installer should correct these as part of the job, and an inspector will usually look for it. If you see any of them on your current heater, fix them now; they are cheap to correct.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
