        <div id="never-cap" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Why You Never Cap, Plug or Remove It
          </h2>

          <p>
            When a T&P valve drips onto the floor, the tempting quick fix is to cap the discharge pipe, plug the valve or replace it with a pipe fitting. People do it, and every plumber has seen it. It turns a nuisance into a genuine hazard. With the valve disabled, a stuck thermostat or gas control can overheat the tank with nothing to stop it, and a tank that ruptures under those conditions can release its contents as steam with explosive force.
          </p>

          <p>
            The drip is useful information. It tells you that your pressure is too high, your system needs an expansion tank, your water is too hot or the valve is worn. All four have straightforward fixes, and none of them involves disabling the safety device. If the drip is causing damage while you sort out the cause, put a bucket under it or run the discharge pipe to a drain properly, and keep it open.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are times to call a plumber rather than going further yourself. Call if the valve discharges hot water or steam in volume, if water from the taps is far hotter than the setting, if the valve keeps opening after you have fixed the pressure and expansion, if the threads in the tank are badly corroded, or if the discharge pipe needs rerouting through walls or floors. These all involve either an active safety problem or work that can make things worse.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the heater is old, rusty around the valve or already leaking elsewhere, the valve may be the least of your problems. Our guide to a <a href="/water-heater-leaking/">water heater leaking</a> helps you tell valve leaks from tank leaks, and if replacement is on the table, our picks for the <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/">best gas water heaters</a> are where we would start. A new heater comes with a new T&P valve, and it is the right moment to add an expansion tank and correct the discharge pipe.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Capping or plugging the discharge pipe</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It is the most dangerous water heater mistake there is. The T&P valve is the last line of defence against an overheated tank rupturing. A drip means something needs fixing: pressure, expansion, temperature or the valve itself. Find the cause, fix it, and leave the discharge path fully open and visible.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Replacing the valve without checking pressure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If the old valve dripped because of thermal expansion or high street pressure, the new one will drip too. Put a gauge with a tell-tale needle on a hose bib and leave it through a few heating cycles before you buy anything. A new valve fixes a worn valve, nothing else.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a valve with the wrong rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A replacement must match the original's pressure and temperature setting and be rated for at least the heater's input. A valve rated for a smaller heater may not be able to relieve enough energy in a real overheat. Read the tag on the old valve and match it, or ask the supply house.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Testing a valve with no discharge pipe</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lifting the lever on a valve with no pipe attached sprays scalding water across the room and at whoever is holding the lever. Fit a proper discharge pipe before testing, keep your face clear, and put a bucket at the end. Watts and other makers make this point in their instructions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the discharge pipe uphill or with plastic hose</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Codes require the pipe to flow by gravity, run full size and be made of a material rated for hot water. Hose, ordinary PVC, reducers and uphill runs restrict flow or fail when hot water arrives. Fix these during any heater replacement or when you notice them.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it normal for a water heater relief valve to drip?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A properly working T&P valve should stay closed. A drip means the valve is responding to thermal expansion, pressure above about 80 psi, excessive temperature, or a worn or fouled seat. Check your pressure with a gauge, add an expansion tank if needed, and replace the valve if pressure and temperature are normal.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I test my T&P valve?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Watts and other valve makers recommend operating the lever manually at least once a year and having the valve reinspected by a licensed plumber at least every three years. The yearly test confirms the waterways are clear. On an older valve that has never been tested, be ready to replace it if it does not reseat afterwards.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What pressure does a water heater relief valve open at?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most residential T&P valves are set to open at 150 psi or 210°F, whichever comes first. The pressure setting must not exceed the tank's rated working pressure, which is usually printed on the heater's rating label. The valve's own settings are stamped on its body or tag, so you can confirm yours before buying a replacement.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I replace a T&P valve myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if you are comfortable with basic plumbing. Turn off the gas or power, close the cold supply, relieve pressure, drain below the valve, unscrew the old valve, seal the threads of a correctly rated replacement and reinstall the discharge pipe. Refill fully before restoring energy. Call a plumber if the tank threads are corroded or you are unsure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a water heater need an expansion tank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If your home has a check valve, backflow preventer or pressure-reducing valve at the meter, the system is closed, and many codes require an expansion tank. Without one, pressure spikes during every heating cycle and the relief valve may drip. A gauge with a tell-tale needle shows whether expansion is driving your pressure up.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where should the T&P discharge pipe end?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Under the IPC it may end at the floor, in an indirect waste receptor, in a drain pan or outdoors. At the floor, it must stop no more than 6 inches above it. It must flow by gravity, be full size, have no valves, tees or threaded end, and terminate where the discharge is visible and will not injure anyone. Local codes may add rules.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a water heater explode without a T&P valve?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. If the thermostat or gas control fails and the valve is missing, plugged or seized, the tank can overheat and over-pressurise until it ruptures. Superheated water then flashes to steam and expands violently. This is rare precisely because T&P valves are required on every tank, which is why you must never disable one.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

